WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.350
So what makes G M smaller?

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.675
Anything that raises G.

3
00:00:04.925 --> 00:00:07.500
Add weight high up, and G goes up.

4
00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:09.750
Passengers in a tower.

5
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.350
Gear stacked on the cabin top.

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.950
The second enemy is sneakier: free surface.

7
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:22.650
Liquid sloshing in a half-full tank runs to the low side when you heel, and it pushes you over further.

8
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.175
It acts just like raising G.

9
00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:30.475
So keep tanks either full or empty when you can, and keep the bilges dry.

10
00:00:30.725 --> 00:00:32.725
Here’s the useful part.

11
00:00:32.975 --> 00:00:35.800
You can feel your stability through your feet.

12
00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:40.350
A quick, snappy roll means a stiff boat, with a large G M.

13
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:47.950
A slow, lazy roll, where she leans over and takes her time coming back, means a tender boat, with a small G M.

14
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:53.975
And if your boat rolls lazier this afternoon than she did this morning, what’s that telling you?

15
00:00:54.975 --> 00:00:56.775
Something changed.

16
00:00:57.025 --> 00:00:58.700
Look for water.
